   "texts": {
    "profile": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: Light In, Sugar Out\n\nINTRO: This is a first pass at photosynthesis: what goes in, what comes out, and where in the plant it happens. Read it once, then try the practice items to see what stuck. Nothing here is scored.\n\nSECTION 1 - The Core Reaction: Carbon Dioxide Plus Water Into Glucose\nPhotosynthesis builds glucose, a sugar, from two raw materials: carbon dioxide and water. The plant cannot run that reaction on its own, because it needs an energy input, and that input arrives as light from the sun. So the reaction has three inputs (carbon dioxide, water, light) and two outputs (glucose, oxygen). The glucose is the point of the whole process; the oxygen is what is left over.\nEXAMPLE: In video game terms, carbon dioxide and water are the crafting ingredients, sunlight is the fuel cost, and glucose is the item you craft. Oxygen is the leftover material that drops out of the inventory. Try it: Name the two raw materials photosynthesis starts from. Answer: carbon dioxide and water.\n\nSECTION 2 - Where It Happens: Chloroplasts and Chlorophyll\nPhotosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves, because that is where the light lands. Inside leaf cells sit chloroplasts, and inside those chloroplasts is chlorophyll, the green pigment that absorbs light. Chlorophyll is what actually captures the sun's energy and passes it into the reaction. A part with no chlorophyll, such as a root, cannot photosynthesise.\nEXAMPLE: You are inking a comic panel of a leaf and you colour it green. That green is chlorophyll inside the chloroplasts absorbing red and blue light and reflecting green back to your eye, so the colour is a side effect of light capture rather than decoration. Try it: Which pigment absorbs the light, and where is it found? Answer: chlorophyll, inside the chloroplasts of leaf cells.\n\nSECTION 3 - The By-Product and the Arrow Chain\nOxygen is released as a by-product: it is not what the plant is building, but it leaves the leaf. Written as an arrow chain, the process is 6 CO2 + 6 H2O, with light, giving C6H12O6 + 6 O2. Read those numbers as molecules, not atoms: six carbon dioxide plus six water make one glucose plus six oxygen.\nEXAMPLE: Picture a basketball game in a gym: the players breathe out carbon dioxide all evening, and the plants along the window take it in and release oxygen back into the room. The plant is running that arrow chain while the game runs. Try it: In 6 CO2 + 6 H2O -> C6H12O6 + 6 O2, which product is the by-product? Answer: oxygen, O2.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: Your comic panel shows a leaf with arrows going in and out, and you want to label it with molecule counts. Before you ink it, check whether 6 CO2 + 6 H2O -> C6H12O6 + 6 O2 actually balances.\n  1. Count carbon on the left: each CO2 carries one carbon, so 6 CO2 gives 6 carbon atoms.\n  2. Count carbon on the right: glucose, C6H12O6, holds 6 carbon atoms, and O2 holds none. 6 = 6, so carbon balances.\n  3. Count hydrogen on the left: each H2O carries two hydrogens, so 6 H2O gives 6 x 2 = 12 hydrogen atoms. On the right, glucose holds 12. 12 = 12, so hydrogen balances.\n  4. Count oxygen on the left: 6 CO2 gives 6 x 2 = 12 oxygens, and 6 H2O adds 6 more, so 12 + 6 = 18.\n  5. Count oxygen on the right: glucose holds 6, and 6 O2 holds 6 x 2 = 12, so 6 + 12 = 18. 18 = 18.\n  6. All three elements match, so the arrow chain is balanced and your labels can be inked exactly as written.\n  ANSWER: 6 CO2 + 6 H2O -> C6H12O6 + 6 O2 balances: 6 carbons, 12 hydrogens and 18 oxygens on each side.\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: 6 CO2 + 6 H2O -> C6H12O6 + O2, so the equation balances. Why: the right side was left with a single O2, so oxygen comes to 6 + 2 = 8, not the 18 sitting on the left. Right: 6 CO2 + 6 H2O -> C6H12O6 + 6 O2 gives 6 + 12 = 18 oxygen atoms on each side.\n\nSUMMARY: Photosynthesis uses light, absorbed by chlorophyll in the chloroplasts of leaves, to build glucose from carbon dioxide and water, and it releases oxygen as a by-product.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. A leafy plant sits in bright sunlight with a steady supply of carbon dioxide and water. Which pair of substances does it produce? [choices: Glucose and oxygen | Carbon dioxide and water | Chlorophyll and light | Glucose and carbon dioxide] -> Glucose and oxygen\n2. A plant is kept in the dark but still has plenty of carbon dioxide and water. What happens to its glucose production, and why? [typed answer] -> It stops, because light is an input and chlorophyll has no light energy to absorb and pass into the reaction.",
    "mirror": "TITLE: Photosynthesis: The Leaf's Recipe\n\nINTRO: Photosynthesis is the plant process that makes sugar food. You will learn the inputs, the energy source, the place, and the outputs.\n\nSECTION 1 - The recipe: inputs and outputs\nPhotosynthesis is how plants make glucose, a sugar they use as food. The plant takes in carbon dioxide (a gas in air) and water. It also uses light energy from the sun (energy is the power to make something happen). The arrow chain is: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen. Oxygen is a gas released as a by-product, meaning it is made along with the glucose.\nEXAMPLE: Example: In a video game, crafting a health item needs two ingredients (carbon dioxide and water) and a power-up (light energy). The health item is glucose; the extra sparkle is oxygen, a by-product.\n\nSECTION 2 - Where it happens: leaves and chloroplasts\nPhotosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves, the flat green parts of a plant. Inside leaf cells (cells are tiny building blocks of living things) are tiny parts called chloroplasts. Chloroplasts hold chlorophyll, the green stuff that absorbs light. Absorb means to take in. Picture: light -> chlorophyll in chloroplasts -> energy for the recipe.\nEXAMPLE: Example: A comic artist colors a leaf green to show chlorophyll. The green color is in tiny chloroplasts inside leaf cells; the leaf is where most photosynthesis happens.\n\nSECTION 3 - What the plant gets and what leaves\nThe plant gets glucose, its sugar food, from photosynthesis. It releases oxygen as a by-product. The plant does not eat soil to get glucose; soil can give water, but not sugar. Sunlight gives energy, not the material the plant is made from. Table: inputs carbon dioxide and water; energy sunlight; place chloroplasts in leaves; outputs glucose and oxygen.\nEXAMPLE: Try it: A comic shows a leaf with green dots. What is the green stuff called? Answer: chlorophyll.\n\nWORKED EXAMPLE: A basketball coach draws a play card for a leaf. The play is photosynthesis. Fill the card: two inputs, one energy source, the place, and two outputs.\n  1. Step 1: Name the main product. The plant makes glucose, a sugar food, so glucose goes on the output side.\n  2. Step 2: Add the two inputs. The plant takes in carbon dioxide from the air and water from the soil.\n  3. Step 3: Add the energy source. The plant uses light energy from the sun.\n  4. Step 4: Add the place. Photosynthesis happens mainly in leaves, inside chloroplasts, where chlorophyll absorbs light.\n  5. Step 5: Add the other output. Oxygen is released as a by-product, so oxygen goes on the output side too.\n  6. Step 6: Check the play card. It reads: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen, in chloroplasts of leaves.\n  ANSWER: The play card: Inputs: carbon dioxide, water. Energy: sunlight. Place: chloroplasts in leaves. Outputs: glucose, oxygen (by-product).\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\nWrong: \"Plants make food from carbon dioxide, water, and soil.\" Why: soil is not an input in photosynthesis; it can supply water, but the recipe's inputs are carbon dioxide and water. Right: carbon dioxide + water + light energy -> glucose + oxygen.\nWrong: \"Oxygen is what the plant uses to make glucose.\" Why: oxygen is on the output side as a by-product, not an input; the plant uses light energy. Right: light energy drives carbon dioxide + water -> glucose + oxygen.\nWrong: \"Chlorophyll is the same as chloroplast.\" Why: chlorophyll is the green pigment that absorbs light; a chloroplast is the tiny part that holds chlorophyll. Right: chlorophyll inside chloroplasts absorbs light.\n\nSUMMARY: Plants make glucose from carbon dioxide and water using light energy. Chlorophyll in chloroplasts of leaves absorbs the light, and oxygen is released as a by-product.\n\nCHECK QUESTIONS:\n1. Hint: Look at the input side of the arrow chain. What gas do plants take in from the air for photosynthesis? [typed answer] -> carbon dioxide\n2. Hint: It is the green stuff in chloroplasts. Which sentence best explains what chlorophyll does? [choices: It absorbs light energy. | It releases oxygen as a by-product. | It takes in water from soil. | It turns soil into sugar.] -> It absorbs light energy.\n3. Hint: Compare the learner's sentence to the arrow chain. A learner writes: 'Plants use oxygen and give off carbon dioxide to make glucose.' What is wrong? [typed answer] -> Oxygen is released as a by-product, not used; carbon dioxide is taken in, not given off."
